1
00:00:06,200 --> 00:00:09,749
What's the simplest bend you can put in a straight line?

2
00:00:09,749 --> 00:00:15,516
It's this one, called ReLU, which stays at zero on the left and then just copies its input.

3
00:00:15,516 --> 00:00:23,233
Now feed it w times x plus b instead of the plain input, and we get one ramp, which is a single ReLU unit.

4
00:00:23,233 --> 00:00:24,949
So where does the ramp bend?

5
00:00:24,949 --> 00:00:30,282
It bends right where the inside hits zero, and that happens at x equals minus b over w.

6
00:00:30,282 --> 00:00:33,333
We'll call that point the knot.

7
00:00:33,333 --> 00:00:39,201
If we turn up the weight, the ramp gets steeper, and with the same bias the knot slides in toward zero.

8
00:00:39,201 --> 00:00:46,466
If we make the weight negative, the ramp flips over, so now it switches on to the left of its knot.

9
00:00:46,466 --> 00:00:51,514
You might wonder why we use ReLU and not the older sigmoid or hyperbolic tangent.

10
00:00:51,514 --> 00:00:58,744
Those curves flatten out for big inputs, so their gradients shrink, and after enough layers the gradient fades away.

11
00:00:58,744 --> 00:01:05,900
ReLU's slope is exactly one wherever the unit is on, so gradients pass straight through.

12
00:01:05,900 --> 00:01:11,509
The idea is that any curve made of straight pieces is just a starting height, a starting slope,

13
00:01:11,509 --> 00:01:13,398
and a slope change at each knot.

14
00:01:13,398 --> 00:01:16,174
The notes write this as equation one point six.

15
00:01:16,174 --> 00:01:23,233
It's a straight line plus one ramp per knot, and each ramp is scaled by how much the slope changes there.

16
00:01:23,233 --> 00:01:27,984
Let's build this curve one bend at a time, starting with just the straight line.

17
00:01:27,984 --> 00:01:32,201
It begins at one half and climbs with a gentle slope of zero point two.

18
00:01:32,201 --> 00:01:38,912
At x equals one, the slope jumps from zero point two to one point four, so we add a ramp that starts right there,

19
00:01:38,912 --> 00:01:42,066
scaled by one point two.

20
00:01:42,066 --> 00:01:46,991
At two point five, the slope drops from one point four to minus zero point eight,

21
00:01:46,991 --> 00:01:50,699
so the next ramp gets a negative weight, minus two point two.

22
00:01:50,699 --> 00:01:58,000
And at four the slope climbs back up to zero point six, which takes one last ramp with weight one point four.

23
00:01:58,000 --> 00:02:02,106
And the reason this works is that a ramp is exactly zero before its knot.

24
00:02:02,106 --> 00:02:09,533
So adding a new ramp can never disturb the part of the curve we've already built.

25
00:02:09,533 --> 00:02:15,173
There's one loose end, because the straight-line piece doesn't look like a ReLU at all.

26
00:02:15,173 --> 00:02:17,572
But it can be written as two of them.

27
00:02:17,572 --> 00:02:21,008
Any number x equals ReLU of x, minus ReLU of minus x.

28
00:02:21,008 --> 00:02:25,966
So one ramp covers the right side and the other covers the left.

29
00:02:25,966 --> 00:02:31,097
Now every term is a scaled ReLU of w times x plus b, and that shape has a name.

30
00:02:31,097 --> 00:02:33,629
It's called a one-hidden-layer network.

31
00:02:33,629 --> 00:02:41,800
Ours needs five hidden units, two for the line and one for each knot, and every weight is read straight off the picture.

32
00:02:41,800 --> 00:02:45,208
Add the five units up, and out comes exactly the same curve.

33
00:02:45,208 --> 00:02:46,629
Nothing was learned here.

34
00:02:46,629 --> 00:02:52,133
We just took the spline and wrote it down as a network.

35
00:02:52,133 --> 00:02:55,950
But real targets curve smoothly, so can straight pieces keep up?

36
00:02:55,950 --> 00:03:00,066
On a closed interval they can, as long as we keep adding knots.

37
00:03:00,066 --> 00:03:04,447
With three knots, which means five hidden units, the fit is pretty rough.

38
00:03:04,447 --> 00:03:07,208
With six knots it's already hugging the curve.

39
00:03:07,208 --> 00:03:14,366
And with twelve, the worst miss is down to zero point zero three, so more ramps really do mean a better fit.

40
00:03:14,366 --> 00:03:16,372
But be careful about what this says.

41
00:03:16,372 --> 00:03:21,387
It's a statement about existence, which means some wide enough network can draw the curve.

42
00:03:21,387 --> 00:03:29,500
It doesn't say your network is wide enough, and it doesn't say gradient descent will ever find those weights.

